## Why TypeScript exists

JavaScript is flexible, which is one of its strengths. A variable can contain a number, then later contain a string, and JavaScript usually does not complain until your code actually runs.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "Types exist only before runtime", "nodes": [{"id": "ts", "label": "task.ts", "sub": "code + types", "kind": "file"}, {"id": "chk", "label": "Type checker", "sub": "tsc · IntelliJ", "highlight": true}, {"id": "err", "label": "Errors in the editor", "sub": "before you run anything"}, {"id": "js", "label": "task.js", "sub": "types erased", "kind": "file"}, {"id": "rt", "label": "Browser / Node", "kind": "client"}], "edges": [{"from": "ts", "to": "chk"}, {"from": "chk", "to": "err", "dashed": true}, {"from": "chk", "to": "js", "label": "compile"}, {"from": "js", "to": "rt", "label": "runs"}]}
~~~

TypeScript adds **static typing** to JavaScript. Static typing means that you can describe what kind of values your code expects, and TypeScript can check those expectations before the program runs.

For example:

~~~ts
let age: number = 31;
age = 32;
age = "thirty-one"; // TypeScript error
~~~

The last line is a problem because `age` was declared as a number.

This is especially useful in larger applications. A team may have dozens of developers working on thousands of lines of code. Catching a mistake while writing the code is much cheaper than discovering it later when a user clicks a button and the application crashes.

TypeScript also gives your editor more information. In **IntelliJ IDEA**, for example, the type of a value can improve autocomplete, navigation, refactoring, and error highlighting.

TypeScript does not replace JavaScript. TypeScript code is compiled into JavaScript before it runs in the browser or in a JavaScript runtime.

---

## Type annotations

A **type annotation** explicitly tells TypeScript what type a value should have.

~~~ts
let username: string = "Alex";
let age: number = 28;
let isAdmin: boolean = false;
~~~

You can annotate function parameters too:

~~~ts
function greet(name: string): string {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
~~~

Here, `name` must be a string and the function must return a string.

You can also use annotations for arrays:

~~~ts
const scores: number[] = [10, 20, 30];
const names: string[] = ["Anna", "Peter", "Sam"];
~~~

Another syntax for arrays is `Array<T>`:

~~~ts
const scores: Array<number> = [10, 20, 30];
~~~

Both forms are valid. The `number[]` syntax is often simpler for basic arrays.

---

## Type inference

You do not have to annotate everything. TypeScript can often **infer** a type from the value you assign.

~~~ts
let username = "Alex";
let age = 28;
let active = true;
~~~

TypeScript understands that `username` is a string, `age` is a number, and `active` is a boolean.

This is called **type inference**.

Inference keeps TypeScript code readable. You generally do not need to write the type when TypeScript can determine it clearly.

For example, this is usually unnecessary:

~~~ts
const name: string = "Anna";
~~~

This is enough:

~~~ts
const name = "Anna";
~~~

However, annotations are particularly useful when defining function parameters, object shapes, public APIs, or values whose type cannot be inferred correctly from the surrounding code.

---

## Primitive types

The basic TypeScript primitive types are familiar from JavaScript:

~~~ts
let title: string = "Learn TypeScript";
let count: number = 10;
let completed: boolean = false;
~~~

There are also `null` and `undefined` values. In a strict TypeScript project, you should think about them explicitly when a value may be missing.

~~~ts
let description: string | null = null;
~~~

This says that `description` can contain either a string or `null`.

---

## Union types

A **union type** allows a value to have one of several types.

~~~ts
let id: number | string;

id = 123;
id = "abc-123";
~~~

A particularly useful pattern is a union of literal values.

~~~ts
type Status = "todo" | "done";

let status: Status = "todo";
status = "done";
status = "cancelled"; // TypeScript error
~~~

A **literal type** represents a specific value rather than an entire category of values.

This is useful for things such as task statuses, user roles, sort directions, or configuration options.

For example:

~~~ts
type Theme = "light" | "dark";
type SortDirection = "asc" | "desc";
~~~

Instead of accepting any string, your function can now accept only the values that actually make sense.

---

## Type aliases and interfaces

When several values belong together, you can describe their structure.

A **type alias** gives a name to a type:

~~~ts
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
};
~~~

An **interface** describes the shape of an object too:

~~~ts
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}
~~~

For everyday application code, both can describe object structures very well.

Interfaces are particularly common when modelling objects and contracts that may be extended or implemented. Type aliases are especially convenient for unions and combinations of types.

For example, this is a natural use of a type alias:

~~~ts
type Status = "todo" | "in-progress" | "done";
~~~

And this is a natural use of an interface:

~~~ts
interface Task {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  status: Status;
}
~~~

You do not need to memorise a complicated rule for choosing between them yet. The important thing is to recognise both forms and understand that they describe types, not runtime JavaScript objects.

---

## Optional properties

Sometimes an object property does not have to exist. Add `?` after the property name to make it optional.

~~~ts
interface Task {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  dueDate?: string;
}
~~~

Now both objects are valid:

~~~ts
const first: Task = {
  id: 1,
  title: "Learn TypeScript"
};

const second: Task = {
  id: 2,
  title: "Build exercise",
  dueDate: "2026-10-10"
};
~~~

An optional property may be missing, so its value can be `undefined`.

You will often see this when working with API responses where some fields are not guaranteed to be present.

---

## Optional chaining and nullish coalescing

**Optional chaining** uses `?.` to safely access something that might be missing.

~~~ts
const city = user.address?.city;
~~~

If `address` is `undefined` or `null`, the expression does not throw an error. Instead, it produces `undefined`.

You can combine this with the **nullish coalescing operator**, `??`, to provide a fallback.

~~~ts
const city = user.address?.city ?? "Unknown city";
~~~

The fallback is used when the value on the left is `null` or `undefined`.

This differs from `||`, which also treats values such as `0` and an empty string as false.

---

## Narrowing types

Suppose a value can be a string or a number:

~~~compare
{"left": {"title": "Unchecked union", "lang": "ts", "code": "function label(id: string | number) {\n  return id.toUpperCase();\n  // ✕ not on number\n}", "note": "TypeScript refuses: a number has no toUpperCase()."}, "right": {"title": "Narrowed", "lang": "ts", "code": "function label(id: string | number) {\n  if (typeof id === 'number') {\n    return `#${id}`;\n  }\n  return id.toUpperCase(); // string\n}", "note": "After the check, TypeScript knows the exact type."}}
~~~

~~~ts
function formatId(id: string | number): string {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    return id.toUpperCase();
  }

  return id.toFixed(0);
}
~~~

The `typeof` check **narrows** the type. Inside the first branch, TypeScript knows that `id` is a string. After the check, it knows that `id` is a number.

You can also narrow object types with `in`:

~~~ts
function getLabel(item: { name: string } | { title: string }): string {
  if ("name" in item) {
    return item.name;
  }

  return item.title;
}
~~~

Equality checks can narrow types as well:

~~~ts
function describe(value: string | null): string {
  if (value === null) {
    return "No value";
  }

  return value.toUpperCase();
}
~~~

Narrowing is one of the most important TypeScript ideas. Instead of constantly forcing the compiler to trust you, you give it a condition that proves which type you are dealing with.

---

## A simple generic

A **generic** lets you write reusable code while preserving the type of the value it works with.

Consider this function:

~~~ts
function first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}
~~~

The `T` is a type parameter. It represents whatever type the caller puts into the array.

For example:

~~~ts
const firstNumber = first([10, 20, 30]);
const firstName = first(["Anna", "Peter"]);
~~~

TypeScript can infer that `firstNumber` is a number or `undefined`, while `firstName` is a string or `undefined`.

The function is still one implementation, but its type information works correctly for many different types.

You do not need to become an advanced generics user immediately. At this stage, understand the main idea: **generics let you create reusable code without throwing away useful type information**.

---

## Types disappear at runtime

This is a crucial difference between TypeScript and JavaScript.

Consider:

~~~ts
const age: number = 31;
~~~

The `: number` annotation helps TypeScript check your code, but it does not exist in the JavaScript that eventually runs.

The compiler turns the code into JavaScript similar to:

~~~js
const age = 31;
~~~

TypeScript types are therefore mostly a **development-time tool**. They help you write safer code, but they do not automatically validate data coming from an API, a form, or another external system at runtime.

This distinction matters. If an API says it will return a number but actually sends a string, TypeScript cannot magically inspect the server response and fix it for you. Runtime validation is a separate concern.

---

## Strict mode

TypeScript's `strict` compiler option enables a collection of stricter type checks that catch more potential mistakes. For new projects, keeping strict mode enabled is generally a good default because it makes TypeScript's guarantees much more useful.

A simplified configuration looks like this:

~~~json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true
  }
}
~~~

You will encounter this setting frequently in real TypeScript projects.

---

## TypeScript in a real project

Imagine a task manager. JavaScript might let you accidentally create this:

~~~ts
const task = {
  id: "123",
  title: "Learn TypeScript",
  status: "finished"
};
~~~

If your application expects `id` to be a number and `status` to be one of a specific set of values, TypeScript can point out those mistakes while you are writing the code.

You can define the expected shape once:

~~~ts
type Status = "todo" | "in-progress" | "done";

interface Task {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  status: Status;
}
~~~

Then functions can rely on that contract:

~~~ts
function completeTask(task: Task): Task {
  return {
    ...task,
    status: "done"
  };
}
~~~

This becomes increasingly valuable as an application grows. Types document what your functions expect, help IntelliJ IDEA provide better autocomplete, and make many refactoring mistakes visible before the application runs.

---

## Common mistakes

### Adding types everywhere

TypeScript can infer many simple types. Do not turn every line into a wall of annotations. Use explicit types where they clarify an API, function contract, or important data structure.

### Thinking TypeScript validates runtime data

A type annotation does not validate JSON received from a server. External data can still be malformed and may require runtime validation.

### Using `any` to silence errors

`any` effectively tells TypeScript to stop checking that value. It can be useful in specific situations, but using it everywhere removes much of the benefit of TypeScript.

### Forgetting that optional means possibly undefined

If you write `dueDate?: string`, code using `dueDate` must account for the possibility that it does not exist.

### Confusing TypeScript with a different runtime

TypeScript does not run directly in the browser as TypeScript. Your build process removes the type information and produces JavaScript that the runtime can execute.

---

## The bigger picture

You can think of TypeScript as a set of tools for describing your JavaScript code before it runs.

Variables can have known types. Function parameters and return values can have contracts. Objects can have defined shapes. Unions can restrict values to meaningful alternatives. Narrowing lets TypeScript understand which possibility you are handling, and generics let you build reusable code without losing type information.

The goal is not to add types everywhere just because you can. The goal is to make the code easier to understand, easier to refactor, and less likely to contain certain classes of mistakes.

In the exercise, you will combine these ideas by modelling tasks with an interface and a literal union type, then writing functions that work with those types.

